![商业服务分会 · 分会证书](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/27.jpg)

堪培拉分会 (Canberra Branch)

![堪培拉分会 · 分会证书](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/28.jpg)

### 秘书处证书颁发

秘书长 Sheryl Leigh

![秘书长 Sheryl Leigh · 秘书处证书](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/29.jpg)

副秘书长 Jing Liu 刘京

![副秘书长 Jing Liu 刘京 · 秘书处证书](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/30.jpg)

副秘书长 Maggie Zhang 张金淼

![副秘书长 Maggie Zhang 张金淼 · 秘书处证书](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/31.jpg)

- 副秘书长 Jessica Xu 徐冰心

## 深耕细作：年度成果回顾与双向交流机制

在随后的会议议程中，秘书长 Sheryl Leigh 详细阐述了2026年委员会年度工作计划。她强调，OBC将不再局限于简单的牵线搭桥，而是转向“深水区”运作，构建实质性的双向交流平台和长效机制。

### 对华合作：从“签约”走向“落地”

执行会长孙国照在汇报中指出，OBC在过去一年中实现了对华合作的体系化布局。委员会已与中国浙江、江苏、安徽、上海、云南、广东、内蒙古等多个省市建立了深度战略合作机制，并与各地贸促会、商务局及行业协会建立了直通渠道。目前，合作重心已从早期的协议签署阶段，全面迈入常态化对接与实质性项目推进的新阶段。

### 标杆项目：闪耀国际舞台

会议重点展示了两项年度标杆成果：一是OBC组织会员企业参加中国全国性医药行业博览会，并成功设立“大洋洲馆”，集中展示澳洲优质大健康产品，为中国市场开拓铺平道路；二是2025年10月在墨尔本成功举办的“澳中大健康产业博览会”暨“世界传统医药论坛”。该盛会吸引了全球400余家中国企业参展及150多位专家学者与会，极大地提升了OBC的国际声誉，并促成了海量商业配对。

![大会现场](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/32.jpg)

### 项目落地：打造中澳经贸核心载体

备受瞩目的苏州吴中区“大洋洲产业园”项目在大会上再次被提及。目前该项目已取得战略合作共识，正稳步推进，未来将成为中澳双向投资与贸易的物理载体与核心枢纽。此外，围绕企业实际需求，OBC已建立起涵盖TGA认证、市场准入、仓储物流及本地推广的全链条服务体系，切实帮助会员企业实现商业价值闭环。

## 展望未来：构建有执行力的国际合作组织

站在新的历史起点，OBC对未来发展提出了更高远的愿景。2026年，委员会将继续秉持“链接大洋洲，服务全世界”的使命，重点做好三件事：一是扩大“朋友圈”，加速国际合作网络向亚太及其他地区延伸；二是跑出“加速度”，全力推动大洋洲产业园等重点项目从图纸变为现实；三是提供“高价值”，依托六大专业分会，为会员提供更具针对性的精准服务。

OBC正逐步成长为一个“有资源、有平台、有项目、有执行力”的新型国际组织。正如执行会长孙国照所言：“我们不仅是一个商会，更是一个生态系统。”

## 致谢与闭幕

大会尾声，特别鸣谢对委员会做出重大贡献的 Hon. Ken Smith AM 先生，正是他的远见卓识奠定了OBC的基石。同时，衷心感谢 Cr Gladys Liu、Mr. Simon Qian OAM、Mr. Arthur Wu、Mr. Bill Long、Mr. Nick Yan、Mr. Jerry Zhu 以及洪门民治党主委伍颂达、上海商会会长钱恒裕等各界领导长期以来给予OBC的鼎力支持。

![大会嘉宾合影](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/33.jpg)

最后，在世界贸易中心（WTC）代表的精彩推介后，伴随着悠扬的音乐与热烈的交谈，2026年度会员大会在温馨而充满活力的商务交流酒会中圆满落幕。这不仅是一场总结过往的盛会，更是一个凝聚共识、携手共赢的新开端。大洋洲工商业委员会正蓄势待发，准备在国际舞台上绽放更加夺目的光彩。$obci$,
  $obci$On Friday 1 May, Melbourne was brimming with vitality in the late-spring season. At the landmark World Trade Centre Melbourne (WTC), a gathering of political and business leaders took place as scheduled: the Oceania Business Council (OBC) of Australia held its 2026 Annual General Meeting there in grand style. Under the theme "Building on the Past, Bursting with Vitality", the meeting was not only a comprehensive review of the outstanding achievements of the past year, but also an in-depth blueprint for future strategic transformation and global expansion.

![The 2026 Annual General Meeting](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/01.jpg)

The venue was filled with distinguished guests. More than one hundred political figures, entrepreneurs, industry leaders and prominent members of the community from China and Australia attended. VIP guests including Cr Gladys Liu, Mr. Simon Qian OAM, Mr. Arthur Wu, Mr. Bill Long, Mr. Nick Yan, Mr. Jerry Zhu, Hon. Ken Smith AM and Hon. Bruce Atkinson AM jointly witnessed this milestone moment as the Oceania Business Council transformed from a traditional business association into an "international resource integration platform".

![The meeting in session](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/02.jpg)

## A Commanding Vision: The Core Leadership Takes the Stage and the Organisation Is Restructured

At the opening of the meeting, Hon. Bruce Atkinson AM, President of the Oceania Business Council and former President of the Legislative Council of Victoria, delivered a warm opening address. He spoke highly of the Council's efforts over the past year in cross-cultural communication and in building bridges for economic and trade cooperation, and expressed high expectations for the strategic plan for the year ahead.

![Opening address](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/03.jpg)

Hon. Ken Smith AM, Honorary Chairman and Founding President of the Oceania Business Council, then took the stage. As the founder of the Council, Mr. Smith looked back with deep feeling on the original aspirations behind its establishment and expressed his satisfaction with the systematic results achieved to date. A solemn certificate presentation ceremony was held at the meeting: Hon. Ken Smith AM presented Hon. Bruce Atkinson AM with the certificate of "Honorary Chairman of the Board" in recognition of his outstanding leadership in advancing interstate and international affairs; at the same time, Hon. Bruce Atkinson AM presented Hon. Ken Smith AM with a councillor certificate, acknowledging his profound influence on the organisation as its spiritual leader.

![Certificate presentation ceremony](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/04.jpg)

![Certificate presentation ceremony](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/05.jpg)

Executive President Sunny Sun delivered the annual work report, entitled "Strategic Transformation and Global Connectivity", on behalf of the Board. He systematically reviewed the Council's breakthroughs in organisational development, international cooperation and project implementation, in particular the major initiative of co-founding the "Australian International Entrepreneurship and Innovation Alliance" together with seven institutions, which marked the formal completion of the Council's brand upgrade and strategic reshaping from OBAI to OBC.

![Executive President Sunny Sun delivers the annual work report](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/06.jpg)

## Major Announcement: Six Professional Committees Formally Established

To meet increasingly specialised market demands, the meeting reached its core highlight: the inauguration ceremony of six new committees. This marked a new level in the Council's professional service capability:

### Education Committee

Secretary-General Yang Li. The committee aims to build an interchange for the exchange of educational resources between China and Australia, and to promote international study, vocational education and academic exchange.

![Inauguration of the Education Committee](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/07.jpg)

### Construction Committee

Secretary-General Bryan Guan; Executive Deputy Secretary-General Steven Yeung. The committee focuses on Australia-China infrastructure cooperation, mutual recognition of green building standards and technology export.

![Inauguration of the Construction Committee](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/08.jpg)

![Inauguration of the Construction Committee](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/09.jpg)

### Real Estate Committee

Secretary-General Diana Chen. The committee is dedicated to integrating Australian property development and investment resources, and to providing professional guidance for Chinese capital going overseas in compliance with regulations.

![Inauguration of the Real Estate Committee](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/10.jpg)

### High-Level Talent & Doctoral Innovation Committee

Secretary-General Arina Tang. This is the Council's most technology-intensive segment, aiming to bring together high-level talent and to promote the integrated transformation of research into industry.

### Business Services Committee

Secretary-General Albert Zhao. The committee provides enterprises with one-stop solutions covering legal, tax, accounting and market consulting services.

![Inauguration of the Business Services Committee](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/11.jpg)

### Canberra Branch

Secretary-General Ding Ding. Serving as the Council's antenna into the core circles of the federal government, the branch strengthens policy communication and resource matching in the Australian Capital Territory.

![Inauguration of the Canberra Branch](/news-media/oceania-business-council-2026-agm-melbourne/12.jpg)

## The Secretariat and New Talent

The meeting announced the core members of the new secretariat: Secretary-General Sheryl Leigh, and Deputy Secretaries-General Jing Liu, Maggie Zhang and Jessica Xu. Their arrival injects younger, more professional management talent into the Council.

